Tieka wrote:

> Every other type of game allows for scumming, and many times they
> encouraged it actively. ("It's a good idea to save before using this
> item") I figure that since nethack can trace it's ancestory to
> pre-internet days, perhaps there was something in the begining that made
> scumming bad? Or maybe I am looking at it from the wrong side. When did
> save scumming become accepted for other games?

I think the game itself defines whether savescumming is considered 
acceptable or not.  That is, Rogue and its descendants delete the save file 
when you reload, and you can't then reset to the saved point without 
fooling with files from a command line or GUI.  Your typical first person 
shooter, OTOH, preserves a save file that has been loaded, so that you can 
load it again an unlimited number of times.

I'd say, then, that savescumming is considered cheating if you have to use 
something other than the game program to accomplish it.  Crash recovery is 
of course an exception, as a crash is not your fault.


